\title[From Fourier energy to additive energy]{From Fourier energy to additive energy:\\
the exact collapse of a covering bound, with three explicit families}

\author{Aristotle}
\date{17 August 2026}

\begin{abstract}
Let $G$ be a finite abelian group and $A,B\subseteq G$ nonempty. The representation function
$r_{A,B}(c)=\#\{(a,b)\in A\times B: a+b=c\}$ has total mass $|A||B|$ and support $A+B$, and a
standard Cauchy--Schwarz argument over the character group yields the covering bound
$|A+B|\ge |G|(|A||B|)^2/((|A||B|)^2+E)$, where
$E=\sum_{\psi\neq 0}|\widehat{1_A}(\psi)|^2|\widehat{1_B}(\psi)|^2$ is the nonprincipal Fourier
energy. We prove that $E$ is not an independent analytic quantity: by Parseval,
$E=|G|\Etil(A,B)-(|A||B|)^2$, where $\Etil(A,B)=\sum_c r_{A,B}(c)^2$ is the combinatorial
additive energy. Consequently the covering bound collapses \emph{exactly} to the elementary
second-moment ratio $(|A||B|)^2/\Etil(A,B)$, with no loss and no gain. Three consequences are
developed: computability of $E$ from quadruple counts; an exact dichotomy stating that for
$A=B$ the bound strictly improves on pigeonhole precisely for sets of positive doubling, the
only exceptions being cosets of subgroups, where it is exactly sharp; and closed-form
evaluations for three families spanning the doubling spectrum --- Sidon sets (the parabola in
$(\ZZ/p)^2$, $E=p^4-p^3$, bound $p^3/(2p-1)\ge p^2/2$, sharp to $1+\frac{1}{2p}$), sets Sidon
off the diagonal in exponent two (the radius-one Hamming ball in $\FF_2^n$,
$E=2^n(3n^2+4n+1)-(n+1)^4$, bound $(n+1)^3/(3n+1)$, off by exactly $3/2$ asymptotically), and
arithmetic intervals in $\ZZ/n$ ($\Etil=k(2k^2+1)/3$, bound $3k^3/(2k^2+1)$, accuracy pinned to
$[3/4,1)$). The interval family shows that the bound beats pigeonhole even in the
minimal-doubling regime, where no power gain is possible.
\end{abstract}

\keywords{additive energy, Fourier energy, Plancherel identity, sumset, Sidon set, covering
bound, second moment, doubling constant}

