\section{Algorithms and complexity}

\textbf{(A) Direct spectral evaluation.} Enumerate the $|G|$ characters, compute
$\widehat{1_A}(\psi)$ for each, and sum $|\widehat{1_A}|^2|\widehat{1_B}|^2$ over $\psi\neq0$:
$O(|G|(|A|+|B|))$ complex operations naively, $O(|G|\log|G|)$ with a fast transform. Needed only
as a check.

\textbf{(B) Combinatorial evaluation.} Accumulate $r(a+b)$ over $A\times B$ in $O(|A||B|)$
exact integer operations, then $\Etil=\sum_c r(c)^2$ and $E=|G|\Etil-(|A||B|)^2$. This is
Theorem~A used as an algorithm and is the recommended route for sparse sets.

\textbf{(C) Closed forms.} For the families above, $\Etil\in\{2k^2-k,\,3k^2-2k,\,k(2k^2+1)/3,\,
k^3\}$ and $E=|G|\Etil-k^4$: cost $O(1)$.

\textbf{(D) Auditing.} To decide whether the bound beats pigeonhole it suffices, by
Theorem~\ref{thm:dich}, to decide whether $|A+A|>|A|$: $O(|A|^2)$ set insertions, with no energy
computation. The numerical value of the gain does require $\Etil$.

\section{Discussion}

\subsection{What the collapse does and does not say}
Theorem~B says that a covering bound obtained by one application of Cauchy--Schwarz to the
nonprincipal spectrum carries exactly the information of the first two moments of $r_{A,B}$. It
does not say Fourier analysis is useless here; it delimits which Fourier arguments can beat it.
Any argument using only $\|\widehat r\|_2$ is subsumed. Arguments using
$\sup_{\psi\neq0}|\widehat{1_A}(\psi)|$, the number of large Fourier coefficients, or higher
$L^p$ norms of the spectrum, use genuinely more and are not subsumed. The identity
$E=|G|\Etil-(|A||B|)^2$ is a diagnostic isolating the point at which a Fourier argument stops
being combinatorics in disguise.

\subsection{The physical reading}
$E$ is the modal energy of the configuration: how much of the data sits in nontrivial modes.
Our identity shows that total modal energy is a coarse invariant --- a count of coincidences ---
which cannot distinguish a set concentrating all structure in one enormous mode from one
spreading the same energy over all modes. Resolving these requires the shape of the spectrum,
not its norm. In exponent two the degradation of the constant from $1/2$ to $1/3$ is a pure
involution effect: since every flip is its own inverse, the $k$ trivial compositions $x+x$ all
pile onto the identity configuration, wasting $k^2$ units of energy at a single point, and the
resulting $3/2$ gap between bound and truth is exactly the accounting cost of that pile-up.

\subsection{The accuracy hierarchy}
Across the doubling spectrum: maximal doubling (Sidon) gives accuracy $\to1$, error
$\le1+\frac1{2k}$; maximal doubling in exponent two gives $\to2/3$; minimal doubling
(intervals) gives accuracy in $[3/4,1)$; no doubling (cosets) gives accuracy exactly $1$ with no
gain. The method's weakness is not inaccuracy but insensitivity: it sees nothing beyond the
first two moments of $r$.

\section{Open problems}

\begin{problem}[Universality of the characteristic defect]
For every finite abelian $G$ of exponent two and every $A\subseteq G$ with $|A|=k$, is
$\Etil(A,A)\ge3k^2-2k$, with equality iff $A$ is Sidon off the diagonal? An affirmative answer
shows that no subset of $\FF_2^n$ pushes the covering bound above $k^3/(3k-2)$, whereas in odd
characteristic $k^3/(2k-1)$ is attained. Both sides are closed-form identities, so this is a
finite convexity question about the distribution of $r$, not a new spectral estimate.
\end{problem}

\begin{problem}[Systematic deficit against Cauchy--Davenport]
In $\ZZ/p$, Cauchy--Davenport gives $|A+A|\ge\min(p,2k-1)$, matched by the interval, while the
second-moment bound converges to $\frac32k$ there. Is $\frac34$ the worst case, i.e.\ does
$(|A|^2)^2/\Etil(A,A)\ge\frac34\min(p,2|A|-1)$ hold for all $A\subseteq\ZZ/p$?
\end{problem}

\begin{problem}[Interpolating families]
Compute $\Etil$ (hence $E$) in closed form for families of intermediate doubling --- rank-$d$
generalised arithmetic progressions, or unions of $t$ intervals --- and decide whether the
accuracy of the second-moment bound is a monotone function of $\sigma(A)=|A+A|/|A|$,
interpolating between $3/4$ at minimal doubling and $1$ at maximal doubling.
\end{problem}

\begin{problem}[Asymmetric pairs]
Formulate and prove the analogue of Theorem~C for $B\neq A$: characterise the pairs with
$(|A||B|)^2/\Etil(A,B)\le\max(|A|,|B|)$, presumably in terms of cosets of a common subgroup.
\end{problem}

\begin{problem}[Beyond the second moment]
Given $\sum r$, $\sum r^2$ and $\sum r^3$, the support admits a lower bound strictly better than
$(\sum r)^2/\sum r^2$ unless $r$ is constant on its support. For Sidon sets
$\sum r^3=k+4(k^2-k)$. Quantify the gain on the three families and identify the spectral
quantity --- necessarily not $\|\widehat r\|_2$ --- to which the third moment corresponds.
\end{problem}

\begin{problem}[Non-abelian analogue]
In a finite non-abelian group the Plancherel formula weights irreducible representations by
their dimensions, so $E$ becomes a dimension-weighted sum of matrix norms. Does the collapse
identity survive verbatim? The dimensional weights suggest not, and the discrepancy would be an
honest measure of noncommutativity.
\end{problem}

\section{Conclusion}

The nonprincipal Fourier energy in the covering bound
$|A+B|\ge|G|(|A||B|)^2/((|A||B|)^2+E)$ is not an independent analytic quantity: Parseval gives
$E=|G|\Etil(A,B)-(|A||B|)^2$, and the bound collapses exactly to the second-moment ratio
$(|A||B|)^2/\Etil(A,B)$. This makes $E$ computable wherever additive quadruples can be counted,
and we have computed it in closed form for Sidon sets (the parabola over $\ZZ/p$, $E=p^4-p^3$),
for sets Sidon off the diagonal in exponent two (the Hamming ball in $\FF_2^n$,
$E=2^n(3n^2+4n+1)-(n+1)^4$), for intervals in $\ZZ/n$ ($E=\frac{nk(2k^2+1)}{3}-k^4$), and for
subgroups ($E=|G|h^3-h^4$). The resulting bounds beat pigeonhole for every set of strictly
positive doubling --- the sole exceptions being cosets of subgroups, where the bound is exactly
sharp --- with accuracy $1+\frac1{2k}$ on Sidon sets, exactly $3/2$ asymptotically in exponent
two, and within the window $[3/4,1)$ on intervals.

